WEBVTT

NOTE Hidden reference scan: jtressle, CC BY 4.0. https://sketchfab.com/3d-models/san-francisco-painted-ladies-cf5aeb7fb0ac4152b43f72ce1dac60d6

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:11.017
Meet Cortex. Same words, different robot behavior. This demo uses synthetic vocal profiles, real MuJoCo physics, and a modeled San Francisco scene.

00:00:11.017 --> 00:00:19.705
Explore the Painted Ladies in three dimensions, with modeled buildings and a bounded walking area.

00:00:19.705 --> 00:00:28.947
Keyboard and voice share the same tools. Turn, walk, or stop. The robot faces its direction before moving.

00:00:28.947 --> 00:00:38.949
First, calmly: Come here. The robot approaches at a moderate pace and maintains personal space.

00:00:38.949 --> 00:00:49.388
Now reset. Come here! The urgent demo profile uses the same words. Priority increases, and the robot walks faster within its safety limits.

00:00:49.388 --> 00:01:00.320
Inside Cortex, synthetic acoustic profiles select visible policies. Live Gradium and Pipecat commands use the same movement tools, with neutral speed defaults.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:08.087
In the demo, a hesitant profile asks for confirmation, approaches slowly, and leaves more personal space.

00:01:08.087 --> 00:01:17.837
Where did you last see my backpack? Cortex retrieves a saved camera frame and its operator-labeled location: beside the planter.

00:01:17.837 --> 00:01:23.840
Take me there. The remembered location becomes a navigation target.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:34.060
Wait! Stop! The fast reflex bypasses planning and stops the robot. The trace separates command acknowledgement from physical settling.

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:42.900
Continue, but slowly. The robot resumes its journey with a lower speed limit. Safety checks remain active.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:53.422
Cortex connects voice, memory, and safe action, with a SambaNova integration for high-level planning. Shared tools turn human intent into robot movement.
